1. What MRUK / Scene actually returns

For the Meta Spatial SDK + MRUK:

Each room entity has:

One or more MRUKPlane components (floor, ceiling, walls, tables, etc.).

A Transform giving that plane’s pose in world space.

For a floor plane:

MRUKPlane.boundary is a list of 2D points (let’s assume Vector2 with fields x and y), and:

They’re in the plane’s local 2D coordinate system.

That system is defined on the plane surface:

Local x and y lie in the plane.

For a floor, that plane is horizontal, so those effectively map to x–z in 3D.

Local origin is at the plane’s local origin (often its center or some anchor chosen by the Scene system).

The boundary is an ordered polygon tracing the perimeter where walls meet the floor.

So:

Boundary point (bx, by) is “this much along the plane’s X/Y axes from the plane origin”.

To get a 3D point in the plane’s local 3D space you do:

(bx, 0, by) if you interpret x as local X and y as local Z.

Then:

To get into your world space, you apply the plane’s Transform (its pose in world).

Crucial: those boundary positions are not already in world space; they are plane local.

2. Two sane strategies

Pick one and stick to it.

Strategy A – Work in plane local, parent under plane (recommended)

Treat boundary points as local positions.

Build walls in plane-local coordinates.

Parent wall entities under the plane entity.

Let the engine apply the plane’s Transform for you.

This is clean and tracks correctly as you move.

Strategy B – Convert to world, parent under world root

Convert each (bx, by) → 3D local (bx, 0, by).

Apply plane Transform (local→world) to get world (wx, wy, wz).

Compute centers and angles in world coordinates.

Parent walls under the same world root as your bone, not under the plane.

This avoids double-transform issues, but you must do the transform math yourself.

